<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#city-state">Glossary</a> · Term · government</p><h1 class="enc-title">City-state</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3500 BCE – present</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The city-state, a city that governs itself and its countryside, was among the earliest forms of state and one of the most inventive. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to Kilwa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> it fostered citizenship, commerce and republican thought, though its small size left it prone to faction and conquest; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, Monaco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-city">Vatican City</a> carry it on today.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to tradition, Sargon began as cupbearer to King Ur-Zababa of Kish, a position that gave him access to the court and administration of one of Sumer's leading <b>city-states</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Assyrian Empire's origins trace back to the city of Assur on the west bank of the Tigris in northern Mesopotamia, which emerged as an independent <b>city-state</b> around 2025 BCE following the collapse of the Third Dynasty of Ur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The new Mexica ruler Itzcoatl formed a coalition with the exiled Texcocan prince Nezahualcoyotl and the <b>city-state</b> of Tlacopan against Maxtla's Azcapotzalco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Old Babylonian Empire grew from unpromising origins: a small Amorite <b>city-state</b> in central Mesopotamia, hemmed in by larger and more established powers, which through patient diplomacy, fortunate timing and the military talent of a single ruler became the dominant power of the ancient Near East within a…</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The decisive transformation from <b>city-state</b> to empire came under Oba Ewuare the Great (r. c. 1440–1473), who seized the throne after a violent succession struggle, curtailed the power of the uzama over the succession, rebuilt Benin City with a new plan, walls and nine gates, and launched the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…and the Cham annals, the Sakkarai dak rai patao, as evidence of several Champas, a confederation of kingdoms and <b>city-states</b> in which, for periods between the 700s and 1471, a king of kings at Indrapura or Vijaya held sway over lesser kings and regional commanders within a mandala.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
